    objectives: ["Recall the main teaching of weeks 1 to 12", "Link each topic to a passage of Scripture", "Prepare for the mid-session test with thankfulness and confidence"],
    terms: [["Stewardship", "Managing what belongs to God."], ["Discipline", "Loving training and correction."], ["Example", "A pattern of living others follow."]],
    intro: "We have spent twelve weeks studying how to raise children in the fear of the Lord. We saw that children are God's gift and trust, that each is unique, that parents teach most by example, that faith is taught in ordinary moments and shared in family worship, that the gospel is for children, that love is the foundation, that fathers and mothers have distinct and shared roles, and that discipline is loving training with clear, consistent boundaries and never cruelty. Today we gather these lessons and prepare for the test.",
    context: "This is the midpoint of the course and the default week for the mid-session test, which covers lessons 1 to 12. Churches may hold the test during class time or on another day. Where it is on another day, this session serves as a guided review. Psalm 127:3 and Deuteronomy 6:6-7 sum up the first half: children are a gift from the Lord, and his words are to be on our hearts and taught diligently.",
    observe: "The first half has laid the foundation of parenting as calling, example, teaching, love and discipline. The second half will address obedience and respect, character, education, friends and influences, communication, hard times, teenagers, protection, gifts and callings, wayward children and releasing children into adulthood.",
    points: [
      ["Calling, knowledge and example", "Children are a gift and trust; each is made in God's image; parents teach by example. Recall Psalm 127:3, Psalm 139:14, Deuteronomy 6:6 and 2 Timothy 1:5. Ask learners to think of one example from daily life and share it with a neighbour. Invite learners to say how this truth could shape one decision this week."],
      ["Teaching, worship and the gospel", "Faith is taught in daily life and family worship, and children can hear and respond to the gospel. Recall Deuteronomy 6:7, Joshua 24:15, 2 Timothy 3:15 and Mark 10:14. Ask learners to think of one example from daily life and share it with a neighbour."],
      ["Love, roles and discipline", "Love is the foundation; fathers and mothers work as a team; discipline is loving, calm and consistent. Recall Psalm 103:13, Ephesians 6:4, Proverbs 1:8, Hebrews 12:11 and Proverbs 15:1. Invite learners to say how this truth could shape one decision this week."],
    ],
    discuss: [
      ["Which lesson has most helped you in caring for children?", "Ask each learner to name one lesson and one change they have made, and tie the answers to the passages studied."],
      ["How would you explain biblical parenting to a young couple expecting their first child?", "Invite simple descriptions that begin with gift, love and example and draw on a verse."],
      ["Which practice from the first half would you most like to strengthen?", "Encourage a specific plan with a time and a person to encourage."],
      ["What questions do you still have about raising children?", "Record them so they can be answered in the second half of the course or by the pastor."],
    ],
    apply: "Write a one-page summary of the first twelve lessons with a Scripture reference for each key idea. Review it twice before the test. Share one lesson with a parent you know and thank God for each in prayer.",
